Active Directory Domain Join Troubleshooting: DNS Resolution Failure (#1)

During an attempt to join a Windows client machine (Client-1) to my Active Directory domain (mydomain.com), a DNS resolution failure occurred. The client was unable to resolve the domain name, which prevented the domain join operation from completing successfully.
When attempting to join the domain through System Properties, the operation failed. The client's system properties showed it was currently configured as a member of a WORKGROUP rather than a domain, with the computer name set to "Client-1" and the full computer name also showing as "Client-1".
To diagnose the issue, the nslookup mydomain.com command was executed on Client-1. The output revealed multiple DNS request timeouts, indicating that the DNS server at address 172.16.0.1 was not responding to queries. The DNS server was listed as "Unknown" in the response, confirming that the client could not communicate with the configured DNS server to resolve the domain name.
First, the DNS service on the Domain Controller needed to be verified and restarted. Next, the Domain Controller's own DNS settings required correction, as it was not properly configured to use itself as its primary DNS server.
The Active Directory DNS zone for mydomain.com needed to be verified to ensure it existed and contained the proper A record pointing to the Domain Controller's Internal adapter IP address.
To resolve the issue, the following steps were performed in sequence. First, on the Domain Controller, the DNS service status was checked using the net start | find "DNS" command, and if not running, it was started with net start dns. 
The DNS service was then restarted after any configuration changes using net stop dns followed by net start dns.
Next, the DNS Manager (dnsmgmt.msc) was opened on the Domain Controller, and the Forward Lookup Zones were examined to verify that a zone named mydomain.com existed. 
If the zone was missing, a new Primary Zone was created with that name. Within the zone, an A record was verified to exist with the name "(same as parent folder)" pointing to IP address 172.16.0.1. 
If missing, this record was created manually.
The Domain Controller's own DNS settings were then corrected by navigating to Network Connections (ncpa.cpl) and accessing the properties of the Internal adapter. In the Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4) properties, the Preferred DNS server was set to 127.0.0.1, ensuring the Domain Controller used itself for DNS resolution. The DNS service was restarted afterward to apply the changes.
To verify the configuration, the command nslookup mydomain.com 127.0.0.1 was executed on the Domain Controller itself, which should return the IP address 172.16.0.1 as the resolution result. If this worked, the Domain Controller was correctly configured.
On Client-1, the DNS settings were updated by navigating to Network Connections and modifying the Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4) properties to set the Preferred DNS server to the Domain Controller's Internal adapter IP address (172.16.0.1). The DNS cache was then flushed using ipconfig /flushdns and the client was registered with the DNS server using ipconfig /registerdns. To confirm the fix, nslookup mydomain.com was executed on Client-1, which should now return the correct IP address.
With DNS resolution working, the domain join was attempted again by opening System Properties (sysdm.cpl), navigating to the Computer Name tab, clicking Change, and selecting Domain under Member of. The domain name "mydomain.com" was entered, and after providing domain administrator credentials, the client successfully joined the domain. A restart was then performed to complete the process.
After completing these steps, several tests were performed to verify the fix. The ipconfig /all command confirmed both machines were on the same subnet (172.16.0.x), and a ping test to 172.16.0.1 from Client-1 confirmed network connectivity. The DNS resolution test using nslookup mydomain.com on Client-1 returned the correct IP address, and the Domain Controller's own internal resolution using nslookup mydomain.com 127.0.0.1 also returned the correct result. The successful domain join was confirmed by the welcome message and the ability to log in with domain credentials.
